runtime/src/vendor — third-party C libraries, vendored
=======================================================

Three std packages are built on C libraries that are carried here, in the
source tree, rather than linked from the system:

    std/tls       BearSSL 0.6            bearssl/   (MIT)
    std/compress  miniz 3.1.2            miniz/     (MIT)
    std/sqlite    SQLite 3.54.0          sqlite/    (public domain)

Each library is compiled ONLY for a program that uses its package, together
with that package's glue (runtime/src/ext/<package>.c), into an archive that
is cached under ~/.cache/voila/ext-<package>-<key>/ exactly as libvoila.a is
under rt-<key>/ (voilac/main.voi: vendor_lib, vendor_build). A program that
uses none of them compiles and links exactly as before; one that does still
depends on libc alone, because none of the three needs anything else.

The compiler carries these files inside itself, as the runtime's own are
carried: tools/build.voi generates voilac/rt/vendor.voi from runtime/src/ext
and this directory (make_vendor_blob). Change a file here, rebuild, and the
new content is what programs get — the cache key is a digest of it.

Nothing in these directories has been modified. Only what is needed was
copied: the C sources and headers, and each library's license. Upgrading
one is: fetch the release into a new, empty directory, check it as below,
replace the files here, and rebuild (./build.sh then ./selftest.bash).


BearSSL 0.6 (bearssl/)
----------------------
Source:   https://bearssl.org/bearssl-0.6.tar.gz  (released 2018-08-14; the
          current release on https://bearssl.org/ when vendored)
SHA-256:  6705bba1714961b41a728dfc5debbe348d2966c117649392f8c8139efc83ff14
          bearssl.org publishes no checksum or signature; the digest above
          was checked against the one nixpkgs pins for the same URL
          (base32 057zhgy9w4y8z2996r0pq5k2k39lpvmmvz4df8db8qa9f6hvn1b7).
Copied:   LICENSE.txt, inc/*.h, and every .c and .h under src/ (the tree
          kept as it is upstream). Not copied: the T0 sources (*.t0, from
          which the shipped .c files were generated), the tools, samples,
          tests and build files.
License:  MIT — bearssl/LICENSE.txt, and the notice in every source file.


miniz 3.1.2 (miniz/)
--------------------
Source:   https://github.com/richgel999/miniz/releases/download/3.1.2/miniz-3.1.2.zip
          (the latest GitHub release of richgel999/miniz, 2026-07-01: the
          single-file amalgamation)
SHA-256:  f0446d863f9c19926ad9483c523fdc42e42b8d4a6a431d27e09d49c79a140d9a
          (matches the digest GitHub publishes for the release asset)
Copied:   LICENSE, miniz.c, miniz.h. Not copied: the examples, readme and
          change log.
License:  MIT — miniz/LICENSE.


SQLite 3.54.0 (sqlite/)
-----------------------
Source:   https://sqlite.org/2026/sqlite-amalgamation-3540000.zip
          (the current release on https://sqlite.org/download.html)
SHA3-256: 7b670a62fdfbd672b75fef004cb703c8a3e87d3a5cc7d675b4a08337004a2d93
          (matches the SHA3-256 sqlite.org publishes for the file)
SHA-256:  68e913b0fe8ec6b4e5f391c594a28872884e80d6cd042a1e50c957be46b5aa4d
Copied:   sqlite3.c only. The glue includes the amalgamation itself, which
          contains everything sqlite3.h declares, so neither sqlite3.h nor
          sqlite3ext.h nor the shell is needed.
License:  public domain — https://sqlite.org/copyright.html. The
          amalgamation's header comment says so; there is no license file.


Build options
-------------
They are set in the glue, in one place each, not on a command line:

  ext/sqlite.c    SQLITE_THREADSAFE=1 (serialized; Voilà tasks are threads),
                  SQLITE_OMIT_LOAD_EXTENSION (no dlopen, so no -ldl),
                  SQLITE_OMIT_DEPRECATED, SQLITE_DEFAULT_MEMSTATUS=0,
                  HAVE_LOCALTIME_R and HAVE_GMTIME_R (thread-safe date
                  functions: tasks are threads), SQLITE_ENABLE_MATH_FUNCTIONS
                  (sqrt, pow, ln … in SQL; -lm is linked anyway), and
                  SQLITE_API=static: every SQLite function is private to
                  the archive, so a program may also reach the system's
                  libsqlite3 through std/ffi without a symbol clash.
  ext/compress.c  MINIZ_NO_STDIO (memory only), MINIZ_NO_ZLIB_COMPATIBLE_NAMES
                  (miniz defines no zlib names, so the system libz may be
                  linked beside it).
  ext/tls.c       none: BearSSL's src/config.h detects the platform. TLS 1.2
                  is the only version enabled.

The extensions are compiled with VENDOR_CFLAGS (voilac/main.voi: "-O2 -w"):
the compiler's default C dialect rather than -std=c11, because these
libraries use POSIX interfaces that glibc hides under strict ISO C, and
without their warnings, which are not the user's to act on.
